INT. GRAIN BARGE "MARIE LOUISE" - GALLEY - NIGHT

A narrow cabin frozen into the river ice. A cast-iron stove ticks. Frost feathers the porthole.

HALVARD KROGH, 60, barge captain, bearded, patient hands, ladles thin soup into tin bowls. His daughter INGRID, 30, wrapped in a army blanket, counts crackers into piles on the table.

TOBY, 10, Ingrid's son, sits cross-legged on the bunk, tracing a face in the frost on the glass.

BRONWEN PELL, 70, a retired schoolteacher with a deaf ear and a sharp one, knits something that is clearly never going to be finished.

HALVARD
Eight days. The ice master said ten at most.

INGRID
He also said the thaw would come on the first.

BRONWEN
(too loud)
What was that?

INGRID
Nothing, Mrs. Pell.

TOBY
Grandpa, the stove's quiet.

HALVARD
It's saving its strength.

A calm settles. The soup steams. Even the wind seems to have gone to bed. Ingrid slides a cracker pile toward her son and he slides it back.

INGRID
Eat, Toby.

TOBY
You first.

A KNOCK on the hull. Three slow raps. Everyone stops.

Halvard sets down the ladle. He takes a boat hook from the wall and opens the hatch. Snow blows in.

A man stands on the ice, hatless, rimed white, a canvas sack over one shoulder. This is the STRANGER, 40s, soft-spoken, smiling with the lower half of his face only.

STRANGER
Forgive me. I saw your smoke from the north bank. I walked a long way.

HALVARD
Nobody walks that ice. It's rotten past the channel buoy.

STRANGER
Then I walked carefully.

Halvard weighs him, then steps back. The stranger climbs down and sits where he is told, nearest the stove.

INGRID
What's your name?

STRANGER
Oskar. Oskar Venn.

BRONWEN
Venn? I taught a Venn. Little boy, wet cough, always drew boats.

OSKAR
Not me, I'm afraid.

HALVARD
Ingrid. Soup.

Ingrid hands Oskar a bowl. He holds it with both hands and does not drink.

OSKAR
Is it only the four of you?

HALVARD
Does it matter?

OSKAR
Only in the way that counting matters.

TOBY
We're not four. There's Gunnar.

INGRID
Toby.

TOBY
He's under the deck.

Silence. Halvard looks at his daughter. She looks at the floor.

OSKAR
Under the deck.

HALVARD
My deckhand. He went through the ice on the third day, going for the line. We brought him back. We have not been able to take him ashore.

OSKAR
I'm sorry.

HALVARD
You're not.

OSKAR
No. I'm not. I'm relieved. I won't have to explain myself to a man who's still looking for his friend.

BRONWEN
(very loud)
What did he say?

HALVARD
Mr. Venn, tell me why you walked out here.

OSKAR
There were nine of us on the grain wharf at the start. We drew straws for who walked the river. The others are sleeping in a warehouse with the doors chained. I did not draw a short straw, Captain. I drew the only straw that said the barge had food.

INGRID
Who told you we had food?

OSKAR
The man who sold you the flour, three weeks ago. He sold it to us as well. He kept a ledger of every boat that bought from him, and every boat that did not leave before the freeze.

HALVARD
You're from the company.

OSKAR
I'm from the harbor. The company is a letter on the door. Nobody's coming for this barge, Captain. Not on the first. Not on the tenth. They wrote you off the day the ice closed, and the river has been kind to them by taking you quietly.

TOBY
Mom?

INGRID
Hush.

OSKAR
I have bread in this sack. And salt. And a lantern, which is how I found you. I came to trade.

HALVARD
Trade what?

OSKAR
Passage. There is a way across that holds. I walked it. I can take two more tonight, three if the child is small and nobody argues.

BRONWEN
Two?

OSKAR
Three.

Halvard lowers the boat hook. Ingrid stands. The stove pops, loud as a shot, and nobody looks at it.

INGRID
Three of five.

OSKAR
Three of five. I did not draw the numbers.

Halvard slowly turns to the hatch, reaches for the lantern, and blows it out. The galley falls dark but for the red slot of the stove.

HALVARD
Then you will wait for morning, Mr. Venn. And in the morning, you will show me this way across. Alone.

OSKAR
(softly)
That is not the arrangement.

In the dark, the sound of a knife leaving a sheath. It is not Halvard's.
